# The POSIX shim is executed by /bin/sh. A CRLF line ending makes the shebang
# unresolvable, and this repository is developed on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true, so the working copy would otherwise get CRLF.
scripts/git-shim/git text eol=lf

# Same reason, for everything else that only ever runs on Linux. install.sh with
# CRLF fails as `env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory`, which is how this was
# found: the installer could not be exercised from WSL against this checkout. A
# systemd unit is worse, because it does not fail loudly: the \r ends up inside
# the value of every directive.
*.sh text eol=lf
systemd/*.service text eol=lf
quadlet/*.container text eol=lf
# install.sh copies this to the config directory and systemd loads it with
# EnvironmentFile, which does not strip \r: PORT=8787 becomes the string
# "8787\r". install.sh's own numeric check caught it, which is the only reason
# it is here rather than in production.
.env.example text eol=lf
